    "narrative": "LawDog enters today\u2019s slate with **Bryce Harper** (77.32) and **Lawrence Butler** (71.08) anchoring the offensive production. **Bryce Harper** is our primary asset against Alcantara; his elite BvP history and favorable park factors make him a locked-in must-start. **Lawrence Butler** benefits immensely from the SP skill adjustment against Lugo, whose 4.57 xERA provides a soft landing for **Lawrence Butler**\u2019s power profile. We are also high on **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (71.12), who remains an efficient engine for RBI and R despite a lower-ceiling matchup against Wilkinson. **Jonathan Aranda** (69.44) is the surprise standout of the day; despite a negative park adjustment, he draws a massive 19% SP skill bonus against a fading Scherzer, reinforcing his role in the UTIL spot.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Braden Montgomery** (47.92), who fell just shy of the 60+ \"Strong\" threshold. While **Braden Montgomery**\u2019s switch-hitting versatility and park factors are appealing, the high efficiency of our projected OF starters pushed him to the pine. Conversely, we are playing it safe by benching **Gleyber Torres** (36.81) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (39.55), both of whom fall well below our mandatory 40-point floor due to brutal matchups against elite arms like Skenes and Messick.\n\nManagers should monitor the lineup cards closely for **Zack Gelof**, **Dylan Beavers**, **Jeremiah Jackson**, and **Lawrence Butler**. Their current scores assume specific lineup spots; should any of them fall to the bottom third of their respective orders, their efficiency scores will adjust downward accordingly. While **Cal Raleigh** (52.20) clears our threshold, his score is tempered by a negative platoon split against the high-velocity sinkers of Dustin May. We remain confident in this high-ceiling lineup, relying on the speed-heavy profiles of **Brendan Donovan** and **Justin Crawford** to bolster our scarcest category, Stolen Bases, even in games where raw power might be suppressed.",
